  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "An ant travelling on the Cartesian plane starts at the origin, and can only go one unit right or one unit up. How many paths can the ant take if it travels:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "to $(8,5)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1287$",
          "worked": "There are 8 right moves and 5 up moves. Choose the positions of the 5 up moves among 13 moves.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{13}{5}\\\\N&=1287.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "to $(8,5)$, and avoids $(3,2)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "727",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Subtract paths through (3,2) from all paths.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "To pass through $(3,2)$, choose 2 up moves among the first 5 moves, then 3 up moves among the remaining 8 moves. Subtract these paths from all paths to $(8,5)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{13}{5}-\\binom{5}{2}\\binom{8}{3}\\\\N&=1287-10\\times56\\\\N&=727.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "to $(10,6)$, and passes through $(4,3)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2940",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Multiply the path counts before and after (4,3).",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Reaching $(4,3)$ requires 4 right moves and 3 up moves. From $(4,3)$ to $(10,6)$ requires 6 right moves and 3 up moves. Multiply the two path counts.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{7}{3}\\binom{9}{3}\\\\N&=35\\times84\\\\N&=2940.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q22-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "to $(10,6)$ through $(4,3)$, but avoids $(7,4)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1540",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p20-q22-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "After reaching (4,3), subtract routes through (7,4).",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "There are $\\binom{7}{3}$ paths to $(4,3)$. From there, $\\binom{9}{3}$ paths reach $(10,6)$. Of these, $\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{5}{2}$ pass through $(7,4)$: first 3 right and 1 up, then 3 right and 2 up.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{7}{3}\\left(\\binom{9}{3}-\\binom{4}{1}\\binom{5}{2}\\right)\\\\N&=35(84-4\\times10)\\\\N&=1540.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      }
    ]
  },
